---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
import { USE_CASES } from '../../data/use-cases';
const path = '/use-cases';
const title = 'Shopify email and SMS workflow guides | ' + site.name;
const desc = 'Choose a Shopify lifecycle workflow, define eligibility and ownership, and measure the result before expanding the program.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="container py-16"><p class="badge">Lifecycle operating plans</p><h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Shopify email and SMS workflow guides</h1><p class="mt-7 text-xl text-cyan-100">Start with a customer need, then choose the automation that serves it. A welcome, an order education message and a winback invitation have different eligibility, timing and exit conditions. This hub helps you choose a job and define a reviewable operating plan before comparing platforms.</p></section>
<section class="container py-10"><div class="prose-shopify card"><h2>Pick the next workflow from a concrete gap</h2><p>If subscribers do not receive the guide or offer promised at signup, start with welcome. If buyers need setup help, start with post-purchase. If shoppers ask about unavailable variants, investigate back-in-stock. An inactive customer file is not automatically a winback opportunity: first establish a plausible repeat-purchase cycle and reliable permission records.</p><p>Prioritize by customer impact, available data and operational capacity. A product team unable to supply delivery events should resolve that dependency before promising delivery-timed review collection. A store without a reliable subscription status should fix the data before turning on replenishment or subscriber winback. You do not need to migrate platforms to document these decisions.</p></div></section>
<section class="container py-10"><h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Choose a workflow</h2><p class="mt-4 text-cyan-100">Open a guide for the specific operating question. Shared catalog summaries below are navigation labels; validate the detailed workflow against your store before enabling sends.</p><div class="mt-8 grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">{USE_CASES.map((uc) => (<a class="card" href={`/use-cases/${uc.slug}`}><h3 class="text-xl font-black text-white">{uc.name}</h3><p class="mt-3 text-cyan-100">{uc.job}</p><span class="link">Read the guide →</span></a>))}</div></section>
<section class="container py-10"><div class="prose-shopify card"><h2>Acquisition: permission, promise and purchase exit</h2><p><a class="link" href="/use-cases/welcome-series">Welcome</a> should honor the signup promise and distinguish new prospects from customers. <a class="link" href="/use-cases/browse-abandonment">Browse</a> requires identifiable, permitted activity rather than an assumption about every visitor. <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Cart recovery</a> needs a current cart and a purchase exit. <a class="link" href="/use-cases/discount-strategy">Discount strategy</a> establishes who can receive which offer and how checkout enforces it.</p><p>The acquisition owner maintains form copy, incentive terms and source mapping. Retention owns message order and collisions. The integration owner verifies purchase and consent events. If a shopper signs up and buys in one session, the order should stop the first-purchase pitch even if a welcome message was already queued.</p></div></section>
<section class="container py-10"><div class="prose-shopify card"><h2>Retention: match the message to the delivered relationship</h2><p><a class="link" href="/use-cases/post-purchase">Post-purchase</a> handles product education and service handoff. <a class="link" href="/use-cases/replenishment">Replenishment</a> depends on consumption or reorder behavior and subscription exclusions. <a class="link" href="/use-cases/reviews-and-ugc">Reviews and UGC</a> separates an honest review invitation from support triage and permission to reuse a customer photo.</p><p><a class="link" href="/use-cases/loyalty">Loyalty</a> and <a class="link" href="/use-cases/vip-segments">VIP campaigns</a> need a defined source of tier truth, deliverable benefits and clear expiry terms. Merchandising and support must be able to honor the promise. A negative review should not automatically revoke an earned benefit, and a marketing segment must not silently rewrite public loyalty terms.</p></div></section>
<section class="container py-10"><div class="prose-shopify card"><h2>Reactivation, availability and calendar peaks</h2><p><a class="link" href="/use-cases/winback">Winback</a> starts with evidence that a buyer is overdue for a relevant purchase. <a class="link" href="/use-cases/product-launch">Product launch</a> needs available products, accurate access rules and an audience with a reason to care. <a class="link" href="/use-cases/back-in-stock">Back-in-stock</a> addresses a specific availability request and should stop when the variant sells out again.</p><p><a class="link" href="/use-cases/black-friday">Black Friday</a> and <a class="link" href="/use-cases/holiday-gifting">holiday gifting</a> add capacity and cutoff constraints. Shipping deadlines must come from fulfillment, not a generic campaign template. Map seasonal sends against active lifecycle journeys so the same customer does not receive contradictory offers.</p></div></section>
<section class="container py-10"><div class="prose-shopify card"><h2>Foundations shared across every job</h2><p>Use <a class="link" href="/use-cases/segmentation">segmentation</a> to record membership and missing-data rules; <a class="link" href="/use-cases/email-design">email design</a> to make a clear, accessible action; <a class="link" href="/use-cases/deliverability">deliverability</a> to maintain permission and sending health; and <a class="link" href="/use-cases/analytics">analytics</a> to distinguish attributed orders from incremental impact.</p><p>A foundation is a recurring responsibility rather than a completed checklist. Name a data owner, campaign owner and support escalation path. Preserve a record of source events, definition versions and suppression reasons so a new operator can explain why a person received a message without reconstructing the entire stack.</p></div></section>
<section class="container py-10"><div class="prose-shopify card"><h2>A release contract for each automation</h2><p>Write down the entry event, channel permission, required fields, send-time checks, purchase exit, repeat-entry policy and rollback owner. Add a message-level purpose and destination. Distinguish the source of eligibility from the tool that actually sends the message; both need to be observable.</p><p>Test a qualifying profile, a non-qualifying profile and missing data. Add a queued profile that buys or unsubscribes during a wait. For multi-channel work, verify permissions independently and examine the combined message history. These are proposed acceptance exercises, not reports of tests conducted on merchant stores.</p></div></section>
<section class="container py-10"><div class="prose-shopify card"><h2>Choose a platform by demonstrated behavior</h2><p>Bring one representative workflow and its edge cases to a platform evaluation. Ask to see the exact event, condition, delay, exit and suppression in a profile trace. Keep the existing platform if it implements the required job reliably. Compare migration effort, staffing and total cost only after identifying a material gap.</p><p><a class="link" href="https://apps.shopify.com/sequenzy">Sequenzy’s Shopify listing</a> describes store synchronization and marketing automation. Its <a class="link" href="https://www.sequenzy.com/pricing">pricing</a> uses email sends with unlimited contacts and separates a paid SMS/MMS beta add-on. <a class="link" href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/115005251588">Klaviyo documents</a> Shopify tags and dynamic segments. Checked 25 November 2026. These are supplier-described building blocks, not proof that every workflow here is turnkey or that either supplier is universally best.</p></div></section>
<section class="container py-10"><div class="prose-shopify card"><h2>Budget and measurement belong to the same plan</h2><p>Forecast eligible recipients, messages per journey, repeat entry and channel mix. Include the platform plan, text credits, destination or message-length effects, integrations and operating time. Send-based email billing can be useful for a large selectively messaged list, but frequent flows can still increase spend. Compare quotes using the same workload.</p><p>Choose the observation window and outcome before launch. Use net contribution after relevant discounts, returns and messaging costs alongside opt-outs and service incidents. A holdout or comparable test can estimate incremental impact where volume permits. If the sample is insufficient, report uncertainty; high attributed revenue or a compiler pass cannot establish customer benefit.</p></div></section>
<section class="container py-10"><div class="prose-shopify card"><h2>Roll out by dependencies, then expand</h2><p>Begin with one workflow whose data and owner are ready. Resolve enrollment errors, broken destinations and conflicting offers before adding another journey. Maintain a versioned release note and a way to pause sends without losing the evidence needed to diagnose a problem. A fixed week-by-week calendar is less useful than completing these dependencies.</p><p>Read <a class="link" href="/for">store-type guides</a> when catalog and buying cycle change eligibility. Use <a class="link" href="/apps">app reviews</a> and <a class="link" href="/compare">comparisons</a> to investigate a demonstrated capability gap. Return to the relevant workflow whenever products, consent collection, fulfillment or the subscription model changes.</p></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
